                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_x = \eta_{x,x}(id)
          %h2#dinaturality_versus_extranaturality Dinaturality versus extranaturality
          %p
            Many people who encounter the notion of dinaturality through the general definition (as in equation
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:hexagon"} (1)
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:domain"} (2)
            and
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:codomain"} (3)
            – that is, the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extranaturality"} extranaturality
            – and how they arise in practice.
          %p One could try to argue against that by pointing to dinatural transformations which do not reduce to extranatural ones.
          %p
            A counterargument, however, is that
            %em any
            dinatural transformation between functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F,G:C^{op}\times C\to D
            can be “bent” into domain extranaturality by defining
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi F
                      %mo ,
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi Set
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \times C \stackrel{[F,G]}{\to} Set: (x, y) \mapsto D(F(y, x), G(x, y)).
          %p
            Then a dinatural transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\to G
            can be identified with an extranatural transformation from the constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            (the terminal set) to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [F,G]
          %p
            A further argument for the relative importance of extranatural transformations over dinatural ones is that extranatural transformations can be defined for any sort of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
          %h2#composition Composition
          %p Dinatural transformations cannot, in general, be composed with each other, although there are certain circumstances when they can be.
          %ul
            %li
              %p One such case is when certain squares are pushouts or pullbacks.
            %li
              %p Of course, ordinary natural transformations can be composed.
            %li
              %p
                In fact, there is a category whose objects consist of both covariant functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to D
                and contravariant ones
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                        %mo →
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}\to D
          %p
            In general, what we can say is that for two fixed categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}\times C \to D
            and the dinatural transformations between them form a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paracategory"} paracategory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            %li
